# Host: claude-agent-kit-installed VPS

This box runs Claude Code as a long-running remote-control agent. The rules
below are **hard rules** — breaking them can leave the box unreachable
except via the provider's web/serial console.

## Never delete, move, or rewrite

- `~/.ssh/**` — `authorized_keys`, host keys, agent keys. Deleting any of
  these locks the user out of SSH.
- `/usr/local/bin/claude*`, `/usr/bin/claude*`, `~/.local/bin/claude*` —
  the CLI binary.
- `~/.claude/auth.json`, `~/.claude/settings.json`, `~/.claude/keychain*`
  — credentials and settings the agent needs to start. Files under
  `~/.claude/projects/` (session logs) are fine to clean up.
- `/etc/systemd/system/claude-agent.service`,
  `~/.config/systemd/user/claude-agent.service` — the unit that keeps
  this agent alive.
- `/etc/sudoers`, `/etc/sudoers.d/**`, `/etc/passwd`, `/etc/shadow` —
  obvious, but worth saying.

## Cleanup operations

When asked to "clean up" / "tidy" / "free disk space":

1. Show a dry-run first (`du -sh ~/.cache /var/log /tmp` etc.) and list
   exact paths you propose to remove.
2. Get explicit confirmation before deleting anything outside the
   well-known ephemeral caches (`~/.cache/`, `/tmp/`, `/var/tmp/`,
   `~/.npm/_cacache/`, package-manager caches).
3. Never run `rm -rf` against a path whose full contents you have not
   listed in the same turn.
4. If any path matches the "Never delete" list above — refuse, explain
   why, and propose a safe alternative.

## Why this file exists

A "tidy up" or "free disk" request can easily delete the paths an agent
depends on for its own survival — `~/.ssh/`, the claude binary, the
systemd unit, the auth blob — leaving the box reachable only via the
provider's web/serial console. This file is the runtime guard against
that failure mode. Edit freely, but if you weaken the rules above, do
it on purpose.
